--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY LibreSSL over OpenSSL (the P-C evaluation, bead vms-2e8)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The OpenSSH port (parent vms-843, docs/design-openssh-port-ovmx.md §3.1) needs a
static libcrypto it can link into the musl-static bootable image WITHOUT waiting
on the post-1.0 OpenSSL-as-OVMX-image (vms-4fa). The design flags LibreSSL as the
likely-cleaner cross-build. Confirmed empirically:

  LibreSSL 4.1.2, in a stock alpine:3.20 (musl) container, built libcrypto.a
  (~11 MB) on the FIRST try with:
      ./configure --enable-static --disable-shared --disable-tests
      make -C crypto libcrypto.la
  — zero source patches, no perl-heavy Configure dance, plain autotools that
  cross-builds against musl out of the box. LibreSSL is OpenBSD's own tree,
  ships alongside OpenSSH-portable, and is what upstream OpenSSH targets.

Plain OpenSSL (Apache-2.0) is the documented fallback (its perl `Configure` +
`no-shared` path also builds static-musl, but it is the heavier machinery). We
did not need it: LibreSSL built clean, so it is the pin.

This lib is the crypto FOUNDATION for the OpenSSH clients (ssh/scp/sftp,
vms-22a) and the SSHD swap (vms-0cd). It is a clean standalone static libcrypto
they link — deliberately NOT entangled with OpenSSH itself (later items). Only
libcrypto is built (OpenSSH uses libcrypto, not libssl/libtls).

Rule 8 note: LibreSSL is permissive OSS — vendoring it is not a clean-room
concern. CLAUDE.md rule 8's HARD INVARIANT covers reverse-engineered VMS
formats/protocols only, never upstream OSS crypto source.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LAYOUT — fetch-vendored, NOT a source copy in-tree
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Unlike third-party/tcc (a full committed source copy), libcrypto is a large,
actively-developed tree (tens of MB); committing it wholesale would bloat the
repo. Instead this is a PINNED FETCH: build-libcrypto.sh fetches exactly this
tarball, verifies it against the SHA256 above (reproducible — a mismatch is a
hard fail), and builds libcrypto.a entirely out-of-tree (default /tmp) inside
the project's containerized musl build tooling. Nothing is installed on the host
and no build product ever lands in the repo (shared-host / Rule: ALL deps
containerized).

  build-libcrypto.sh   fetch (SHA256-verified) + configure static + build
                       libcrypto.a into $WORK (default /tmp/ovmx-libcrypto).
  test/hello-crypto.c  tiny probe: SHA256 known-answer test against libcrypto.a.
  test/run_hello_crypto.sh
                       build the lib, then compile+static-link+run the probe and
                       assert the SHA-256 known-answer — the acceptance for this
                       bead. Runs in CI (job: libcrypto-static-musl).

To move the pin:
  1. Pick a new libressl-X.Y.Z.tar.gz from the OpenBSD LibreSSL directory.
  2. Fetch its SHA256 from that directory's SHA256 file.
  3. Update Pinned version / Tarball SHA256 / URLs above and the
     LIBRESSL_VERSION / LIBRESSL_SHA256 defaults in build-libcrypto.sh.
  4. Re-run third-party/libcrypto/test/run_hello_crypto.sh in the musl
     container to reconfirm the probe still passes at the new pin.
